      "alerts": [
        {
          "event": "Beach Hazards Statement",
          "headline": "Beach Hazards Statement issued October 12 at 3:51AM PDT until October 12 at 10:00PM PDT by NWS San Francisco CA",
          "severity": "Moderate",
          "urgency": "Expected",
          "description": "* WHAT...For the Coastal Flood Advisory, Up to 1.5 ft of\ninundation above ground level is possible in low-lying areas\nnear shorelines and tidal waterways. For the Beach Hazards\nStatement, increased risk of rip currents and sneaker waves\nexpected with breaking waves up to 16 feet.\n\n* WHERE...San Francisco, Northern Monterey Bay and Southern\nMonterey Bay and Big Sur Coast Counties.\n\n* WHEN...For the Coastal Flood Advisory, until 3 PM PDT Tuesday.\nFor the Beach Hazards Statement, through Monday evening.\n\n* IMPACTS...Flooding of lots, parks, and roads with only\nisolated road closures expected. High tides will result in\nflooding along coastal trails, cut off access to some low-\nlying beaches and coastal areas, and bring water and waves\nfarther up the beach than typical. Dangerous conditions are\nforecast along the shoreline. Hazards include sneaker waves,\nstrong rip currents, and large breaking waves.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...At the San Francisco tidal gauge, high\ntide is expected to be 1.5 ft above normal (7.3 ft MLLW) at\n12:46 PM Monday 10/12, and 1.4 ft above normal (7.2 ft MLLW)\nat 1:14 PM Tuesday 10/13. These predictions include up to 1.5\nft of surge that will enhance the astronomical tide and\nflooding threat. High tide varies up to 90 minutes earlier or\nlater along the Pacific Coast and through the San Francisco\nBay, respectively.",
          "instruction": "If travel is required, allow extra time as some roads may be\nclosed. Do not drive around barricades or through water of\nunknown depth. Take the necessary actions to protect flood-prone\nproperty.\n\nStay off of jetties, piers, rocks, and other waterside\ninfrastructure. Remain out of the water to avoid hazardous surf\nand NEVER turn your back on the ocean. Monitor local weather,\nsurf and tide forecasts at www.weather.gov/mtr.",
          "expires": "2026-10-12T15:00:00-07:00",
          "source_url": "https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.ea16399f9c72849312dcd2cf9f1815fc4399e83a.001.2"
        },
        {
          "event": "Coastal Flood Advisory",
          "headline": "Coastal Flood Advisory issued October 12 at 3:51AM PDT until October 13 at 3:00PM PDT by NWS San Francisco CA",
          "severity": "Minor",
          "urgency": "Expected",
          "description": "* WHAT...For the Coastal Flood Advisory, Up to 1.5 ft of\ninundation above ground level is possible in low-lying areas\nnear shorelines and tidal waterways. For the Beach Hazards\nStatement, increased risk of rip currents and sneaker waves\nexpected with breaking waves up to 16 feet.\n\n* WHERE...San Francisco, Northern Monterey Bay and Southern\nMonterey Bay and Big Sur Coast Counties.\n\n* WHEN...For the Coastal Flood Advisory, until 3 PM PDT Tuesday.\nFor the Beach Hazards Statement, through Monday evening.\n\n* IMPACTS...Flooding of lots, parks, and roads with only\nisolated road closures expected. High tides will result in\nflooding along coastal trails, cut off access to some low-\nlying beaches and coastal areas, and bring water and waves\nfarther up the beach than typical. Dangerous conditions are\nforecast along the shoreline. Hazards include sneaker waves,\nstrong rip currents, and large breaking waves.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...At the San Francisco tidal gauge, high\ntide is expected to be 1.5 ft above normal (7.3 ft MLLW) at\n12:46 PM Monday 10/12, and 1.4 ft above normal (7.2 ft MLLW)\nat 1:14 PM Tuesday 10/13. These predictions include up to 1.5\nft of surge that will enhance the astronomical tide and\nflooding threat. High tide varies up to 90 minutes earlier or\nlater along the Pacific Coast and through the San Francisco\nBay, respectively.",
          "instruction": "If travel is required, allow extra time as some roads may be\nclosed. Do not drive around barricades or through water of\nunknown depth. Take the necessary actions to protect flood-prone\nproperty.\n\nStay off of jetties, piers, rocks, and other waterside\ninfrastructure. Remain out of the water to avoid hazardous surf\nand NEVER turn your back on the ocean. Monitor local weather,\nsurf and tide forecasts at www.weather.gov/mtr.",
